<h1>Authorization checks</h1>
<p class="lede">You can answer "can this user do this?" in two ways: with the <b>roles and scopes</b> in the token, or at record level with the
    <b>authorization schema and the AuthZ API</b>.</p>

<h2 id="which">Which one should I use?</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>General rules like "managers can delete orders"</td><td>Roles and scopes — reading the token is enough.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Ali can only edit his own team's documents"</td><td>Authorization schema (ReBAC) + AuthZ API.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sharing, folder / project hierarchies, resource ownership</td><td>Authorization schema (ReBAC) + AuthZ API.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="roles">Roles and scopes</h2>
<p>The roles and scopes you define in a pool are written into the access token. After validating the token, your API looks at the claims:</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Access token (excerpt)</span><pre>{
  "sub": "6f1c…",
  "aud": "https://api.company.com",
  "scope": "openid orders:read orders:write",
  "role": ["manager"]
}</pre></div>

<h2 id="schema">Authorization schema (ReBAC)</h2>
<p>On the <b>Authorization Schema</b> screen in the pool menu:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Define <b>resource types</b>, e.g. <code>folder</code> and <code>document</code>.</li>
    <li>Add <b>links</b> between types, e.g. every document has a folder.</li>
    <li>Define <b>permissions</b>: granted directly (e.g. <code>editor</code>), included from another permission (editors can view) or inherited
        through a link (a folder's editor is also an editor of its documents).</li>
    <li><b>Publish</b> the schema. Schemas are versioned: draft, live, inactive. You can start from the built-in Owner / Editor / Viewer template.</li>
</ol>
<p>On the <b>Relations</b> screen you grant users permissions on records and link records to each other. On the <b>Access Check</b> screen you
    can test which permissions a user has on a record.</p>

<h2 id="api">AuthZ API</h2>
<p>Your application (usually your back end) calls the AuthZ API with a Bearer token. The token's audience must be
    <code>https://auth.company.com/authz</code>: add the AuthZ address to the application's allowed APIs and pass it as <code>resource</code> when
    requesting the token. The token only authenticates the <b>caller</b>; the user being checked is in the request body.</p>
<p><b>Which pool can be queried:</b> <code>isolationId</code> must be the pool that issued the token (an access token of one of that pool's
    users). A token from another pool is rejected with <code>403</code> (<code>authz.query_not_allowed</code>). Console users and pool service
    accounts (management pool) can query a pool only if they have at least <b>viewer</b> access to it. This applies to <code>check</code>,
    <code>batch-check</code> and all <code>lookup</code> endpoints.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Permission check</span><pre>POST https://auth.company.com/authz/check
Authorization: Bearer &lt;access token&gt;
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isolationId": "&lt;pool id&gt;",
  "user": "user:&lt;user sub&gt;",
  "relation": "editor",
  "object": "document:order-2026-001"
}</pre></div>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Response</span><pre>{ "data": { "allowed": true } }</pre></div>
<p>Other endpoints:</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>POST /authz/batch-check</code></td><td>Several checks in one request (a <code>checks</code> list, each with a <code>correlationId</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /authz/lookup/by-user</code></td><td>Records a user can access.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /authz/lookup/by-object</code></td><td>Users who can access a record.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /authz/lookup/my-access</code></td><td>A user's access across the given types.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST / DELETE /authz/relations</code></td><td>Add / remove relations; for the management panel. To write relations from your
            application, use the <a href="/en/docs/pool-apis">Pool APIs</a>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout tip"><p><b>Tip.</b> When a record is created (e.g. a new document), write its owner and folder from your back end with a service account via
    <a href="/en/docs/pool-apis"><code>/idp/apis/relations</code></a>; every later check is computed from these relations.</p></div>
